  "description": "Center and whiten feature distributions so transformed variables possess zero mean and an identity covariance matrix.\n\nWhitening eliminates linear feature correlations and equalizes variances across all principal directions, widely used in image feature representations.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\tilde{X} = (X - \\mu) W_{\\text{ZCA}}, \\quad W_{\\text{ZCA}} = V \\Lambda^{-1/2} V^T\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Center data matrix by subtracting feature-wise sample means\n2. Compute sample covariance matrix and its spectral eigendecomposition\n3. Apply symmetric ZCA transformation matrix to sphericize covariance to identity\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'whitened_data' and 'whitening_matrix'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Whitening Transform\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of machine learning. Data samples, image pixels, and word embeddings are all represented as multidimensional vectors and matrices. **Whitening Transform** transforms coordinate systems, extracts principal axes of variation, or decomposes complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nWhitening eliminates linear feature correlations and equalizes variances across all principal directions, widely used in image feature representations. In this problem, the goal is to: center and whiten feature distributions so transformed variables possess zero mean and an identity covariance matrix.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\tilde{X} = (X - \\mu) W_{\\text{ZCA}}, \\quad W_{\\text{ZCA}} = V \\Lambda^{-1/2} V^T\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Center data matrix by subtracting feature-wise sample means\n2. **Phase 2**: Compute sample covariance matrix and its spectral eigendecomposition\n3. **Phase 3**: Apply symmetric ZCA transformation matrix to sphericize covariance to identity\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Feature Compression & Projections**: Operations like low-rank approximations and orthogonal projections compress high-dimensional feature spaces while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Numerical Stability**: When inverting matrices or solving linear systems, ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with very small determinants or high condition numbers) require regularization to prevent numerical instability.\n* **Vectorized Execution**: Expressing computations as matrix multiplications allows GPUs and CPUs to leverage hardware tensor cores for maximum throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: X = [[1.0, 2.0], [2.0, 4.0], [3.0, 5.0], [4.0, 8.0]]\n\nOutput: {\"whitened_data\": [[-0.8487, -0.8201], [-0.5625, -0.0712], [1.4012, -0.5354], [0.0099, 1.4268]], \"whitening_matrix\": [[3.6411, -1.6775], [-1.6775, 1.2132]]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated la_whitening under standard test conditions."
